---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/agentsview/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/agentsview/',
    name: 'sitrep vs AgentsView - session logs or project state',
    description:
      'An honest comparison of sitrep and AgentsView: analytics over the session transcripts your coding agents leave behind - what ran, what it cost - versus a status layer parsed from the markdown they wrote - where projects stand and what is waiting on you.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI project status dashboard - a passive state layer over existing files',
    other: 'Session analytics for coding agents - dashboards over the transcripts they leave on disk',
  },
  {
    dim: 'What it reads',
    sitrep: 'The markdown agents wrote: plans, analyses, decision logs, progress notes',
    other: 'The logs agents left: session transcripts - prompts, tool calls, tokens - across many agent CLIs',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Question answered',
    sitrep: '"Where do my projects stand, and what is waiting on me?" - forward-looking',
    other: '"What did my agents do, when, and what did it cost?" - retrospective',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Format demanded',
    sitrep: 'None - reads what is already there',
    other: 'None - reads what is already there',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Scope',
    sitrep: 'Cross-project, across every repo and workspace',
    other: 'Cross-project, across every indexed agent and repo',
  },
  {
    dim: 'State surfaced',
    sitrep: 'Status, phases, open decisions, due dates, staleness against the repo',
    other: 'Activity, heatmaps, cost and token spend, session summaries and search',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Writes to your files',
    sitrep: 'Three guarded state operations in the document’s own convention; nothing else, ever',
    other: 'Nothing - read-only over the logs',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Agent integration',
    sitrep: 'MCP server exposing parsed state, read-only first',
    other: 'Reads the session files of many agent CLIs; the agents are the subject, not the client',
  },
  {
    dim: 'License and price',
    sitrep: 'Commercial desktop app, in development',
    other: 'Free, MIT-licensed open source',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Local-first',
    sitrep: 'Yes - no account, no network, no telemetry by default',
    other: 'Yes by default - indexes locally on your machine',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs AgentsView - session logs or project state"
  description="AgentsView and sitrep are the closest neighbors in this space: both local-first, both passive, both cross-project. The difference is the source of truth - AgentsView reads the session logs your agents leave (activity and cost); sitrep reads the markdown they wrote (status and decisions)."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        <Sitrep /> vs AgentsView
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        Of every tool on these comparison pages, AgentsView is the closest
        neighbor: free, open-source, local-first, passive, cross-project -
        the same posture <Sitrep /> holds on every axis. The difference is what
        each one reads, and therefore what question it can answer. For "what
        did my agents actually do?", AgentsView is the better tool, plainly.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by <a href="/about/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">the maker of sitrep</a>. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "use AgentsView", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Your coding agents leave two trails behind. One is the session
          transcript - every prompt, tool call and token, logged as data.
          The other is the markdown they wrote on purpose - plans, analyses,
          decision logs, progress notes. AgentsView indexes the first trail
          and turns it into activity dashboards: what ran, when, at what
          cost. <Sitrep /> parses the second and turns it into a status view:
          where each project stands, what is stalled, what is waiting on
          you.
        </p>
        <p>
          One is telemetry, the other is state. Telemetry tells you the
          agent worked for three hours on Tuesday; state tells you the
          migration is at phase 2 of 5 and blocked on a decision only you
          can make.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"><Sitrep /></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">AgentsView</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-av">
      <h2 id="choose-av" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose AgentsView</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want to see and search what your agents did - sessions,
          timelines, heatmaps - across many different agent CLIs.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Token and cost tracking matters: you want spend per project and
          per agent, measured from the logs.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You need to find a past session - what was asked, what was tried -
          and replay the trail.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want free, MIT-licensed open source you can run today.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Session analytics is not on the roadmap - dashboards over
        transcripts are AgentsView's job. The most <Sitrep /> would ever take
        from the session trail is a timestamp: evidence that a project
        moved, feeding its staleness signals - never a surface built over
        the transcripts themselves.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose <Sitrep /></h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          The question is where things stand, not what happened: status,
          phases, open decisions, due dates - parsed from the docs
          themselves.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Re-entry is the pain: opening a cold project after weeks and
          needing the state, not the history.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You care whether the docs still tell the truth - staleness against
          repo activity is a signal you want surfaced, not something you
          reconstruct from timelines.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Your state lives in markdown that no session produced - notes from
          other tools, hand-written decision logs, docs from before the
          agents arrived.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Using both</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        This is the most genuinely complementary pairing on these pages:
        same machine, same repos, two different trails read for two
        different questions. Run
        AgentsView to know what your agents did and what it cost;
        run <Sitrep /> to know where your projects stand and what needs you. Neither asks
        you to change anything about how you or your agents work, and both
        keep everything
        <a href="/security/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">on your machine</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-faint">
        <Sitrep /> is in development, launching first on macOS. Local-first -
        your files never leave your machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
